---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import MemoryHierarchy from '../components/MemoryHierarchy';
import StressGauge from '../components/StressGauge';
import MemoryFlow from '../components/diagrams/MemoryFlow.astro';
import DecayCurve from '../components/diagrams/DecayCurve';
import PraxisRouting from '../components/diagrams/PraxisRouting.astro';
import CircuitBreaker from '../components/diagrams/CircuitBreaker.astro';
import StressMath from '../components/diagrams/StressMath.astro';
import SensoryFlow from '../components/diagrams/SensoryFlow.astro';
import Capability from '../components/diagrams/Capability.astro';
import Mlfq from '../components/diagrams/Mlfq.astro';
import TokenPipe from '../components/diagrams/TokenPipe.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Subsystems — AnimaOS"
  description="Detailed specifications for memory, praxis, interoception, senses, and security subsystems."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="02 — Subsystems"
    title="Memory, praxis, interoception, senses, security."
    description="Each subsystem is specified by intent, mechanism, and code shape. The verification posture is enforced at the crate level via lib.rs attributes and checked in CI."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Memory · CLS hierarchy</span>
        <h2>Three tiers, derived from cognitive neuroscience.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The memory subsystem implements a three-tier model derived from the
          Complementary Learning Systems framework. The justification for three
          tiers — rather than the conventional two-tier cache + storage — is that
          the working context of an LLM has fundamentally different access
          patterns from a recency cache.
        </p>
      </div>

      <MemoryHierarchy client:visible />

      <div style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <MemoryFlow />
      </div>

      <div style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <DecayCurve client:visible />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Scheduler · MLFQ</span>
        <h2>Three priority tiers with iteration-aware batching.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Tasks are dispatched at the iteration level — token-by-token — rather
          than as monolithic jobs. Per-task token slices are tracked; tasks decay
          to lower tiers as their slice exhausts, and a periodic boost prevents
          starvation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <Mlfq />

      <div style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <TokenPipe />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Praxis · efferent actuator</span>
        <h2>Tools as files. Routing by relative score.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Praxis is the agent's motor cortex. External toolsets, hardware engines,
          and MCP/A2A endpoints are exposed as device drivers under
          <code>/dev/anima/praxis/tools/</code>. Where <code>senses</code> is
          afferent, <code>praxis</code> is efferent.
        </p>
      </div>

      <PraxisRouting />

      <div style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <CircuitBreaker />
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Length-robust relative routing</h3>
          <p>
            A naive tool router selects tools whose score against the current
            query exceeds a fixed threshold. This breaks down two ways: marginal
            tools accumulate and dilute context; specific queries lose good tools
            below the threshold.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`T_filtered = { t ∈ T |
    score(t, q) ≥ τ_rel · max score(t', q) }

// default τ_rel = 0.7`}</code></pre>
          <p class="muted small">
            The filter behaves consistently across registry sizes: a small registry
            surfaces a small number of close matches; a large registry surfaces the
            same close matches and prunes the long tail.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>Circuit breakers per tool</h3>
          <p>
            Every tool driver is wrapped in a state monitor that observes failure
            rates and trips when the failure pattern indicates the tool is
            unhealthy. The breaker isolates the failure from the system core.
          </p>
          <div class="cb-states">
            <div class="cb-state ok"><strong>Closed</strong><span>requests pass through; failures counted</span></div>
            <div class="cb-state warn"><strong>Open</strong><span>requests fail fast; cooldown timer running</span></div>
            <div class="cb-state probe"><strong>HalfOpen</strong><span>single probe request; success returns to Closed</span></div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>Wasmtime sandbox</h3>
          <p>
            Untrusted code generated by the agent — or downloaded as a tool
            implementation — runs in a capability-secured wasmtime instance with
            gas metering, memory bounds, and capability-typed imports. A
            misbehaving tool cannot exhaust system resources.
          </p>
          <ul class="cb-list">
            <li>Gas metering per invocation</li>
            <li>Memory cap enforced by the runtime</li>
            <li>Capability-typed imports — no implicit access</li>
            <li>Shared runtime instance, lazy initialisation</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>MCP and A2A as remote drivers</h3>
          <p>
            Model Context Protocol servers appear as files under
            <code>/dev/anima/praxis/tools/mcp/&lt;server&gt;/</code>. Peer agents
            speaking the Agent-to-Agent protocol appear under
            <code>/dev/anima/praxis/tools/a2a/&lt;peer&gt;/</code>.
          </p>
          <p class="muted small">
            From the agent's perspective, these are tools like any other —
            discoverable, scoreable, breakable. The transport is invisible above
            the praxis driver layer.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Interoception · the felt sense</span>
        <h2>One scalar drives the lifecycle.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The stress index combines two pressures into a single normalised scalar:
          latency degradation (TTFT ratio versus baseline) and context saturation
          (active L1 tokens versus capacity). The output is visible on the
          telemetry stream at 1 Hz and crosses thresholds that drive state
          transitions in <code>vita</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <StressMath />

      <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Composite stress formula</h3>
          <pre><code>{`s = clamp(0, 1,
    β · (TTFT / TTFT_baseline - 1) +
    (1 - β) · (active_tokens / max_tokens)
)

// default β = 0.5 (equal weight)`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            The rolling TTFT window smooths transient spikes. The context ratio
            is exact. The β parameter is tuneable per workload, but the default
            balances latency and memory pressure equally.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Threshold map</h3>
          <StressGauge client:visible />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Senses · afferent input</span>
        <h2>Text, voice, RPC — every signal is sensory.</h2>
      </div>

      <SensoryFlow />

      <div class="grid grid-3" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <article class="card sense-card">
          <span class="sense-icon">⌨</span>
          <h3>Text</h3>
          <p>
            UTF-8 buffers from <code>/dev/anima/senses/human</code>. Parsed into
            <code>SensoryPacket</code>s with source, timestamp, priority.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card sense-card">
          <span class="sense-icon">🎙</span>
          <h3>Voice (PCM)</h3>
          <p>
            Streaming 16-bit PCM through VAD into a local speech-to-text pipeline.
            High priority by default — voice signals an attentive human.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card sense-card">
          <span class="sense-icon">⇄</span>
          <h3>Peers (A2A)</h3>
          <p>
            Optional peer-agent streams under <code>/dev/anima/senses/peers/</code>.
            Each peer is a sensory node; the priority tag is driver-level.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Self · capability barrier</span>
        <h2>Typestate at compile time. Roles at boot.</h2>
      </div>

      <Capability />

      <div class="card" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <p>
          The capability system uses Rust's typestate pattern to make permission
          violations un-compilable. A <code>Capability&lt;Unverified&gt;</code>
          cannot be passed to a function expecting
          <code>Capability&lt;Verified&gt;</code>; the type system enforces the
          ordering at compile time.
        </p>
        <pre><code>{`// crates/self/src/cap.rs
pub struct Capability<S> {
    inner: CapInner,
    _state: PhantomData<S>,
}

pub struct Unverified;
pub struct Verified;

impl Capability<Unverified> {
    pub fn verify(self, role: &Role) -> Result<Capability<Verified>, CapErr> {
        // …role-bound check; consumes self
    }
}

// Only \`Capability<Verified>\` can authorise syscalls.
pub fn syscall(cap: Capability<Verified>, op: SyscallEnum) -> Result<()> { /* … */ }`}</code></pre>
        <p class="muted small">
          Roles are fixed at build time. Elevation tokens are single-use values
          that upgrade a restricted capability to an unrestricted one for exactly
          one call.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .section-head {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }
  .section-head h2 { margin: 1rem 0; }
  .lede {
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .cb-states {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .cb-state {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 100px 1fr;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem;
    background: var(--bg-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
    border-radius: 6px;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
  .cb-state strong { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .cb-state span { color: var(--fg-2); font-size: 0.82rem; }
  .cb-state.ok { border-left: 2px solid var(--accent); }
  .cb-state.warn { border-left: 2px solid var(--warn); }
  .cb-state.probe { border-left: 2px solid var(--accent-2); }

  .cb-list {
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
  }
  .cb-list li {
    color: var(--fg-1);
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    padding: 0.25rem 0;
  }

  .sense-card { text-align: center; }
  .sense-icon {
    font-size: 2rem;
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .sense-card h3 { margin: 0.5rem 0; }
  .sense-card p { font-size: 0.9rem; }
</style>
